  "prediction": "**Mountain Walker Dies in Snowdonia After Falling from Tryfan Peak**\n\nA 53-year-old woman, Jane Wilson, fell to her death while attempting to find a safe route down from the Tryfan peak in Snowdonia, North Wales. The incident occurred on March 25, when Wilson and her husband, Gary, decided not to reach the summit due to the approaching dusk.\n\nUsing Gary's smartphone to navigate, they headed across the mountain's west face, which is known for its loose rocks and steep inclines. Jane Wilson, an experienced mountain walker and academic engagement librarian at the University of Manchester, fell 20-30 feet down a vertical cliff and then tumbled about 150 meters into a rocky area called Notch.\n\nThe coroner's inquest heard that the couple had been using a smartphone app to find their route, rather than a traditional map and compass. The inquest also revealed that the west face of Tryfan is a challenging and hazardous route, particularly for inexperienced walkers.\n\nDetective Constable Tim Bird, leader of the Ogwen Valley Mountain Rescue Team, stated that the couple's decision to traverse the west face was a mistake, as it would have been safer to retreat the way they came or take the North Ridge route. Bird also emphasized the importance of carrying a paper map and compass, rather than relying solely on electronic navigation aids.\n\nThe coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death, highlighting the risks associated with mountain walking and the importance of proper planning and preparation.\n\n**Mountain Rescue Team Warns of Dangers of Smartphone Navigation**\n\nThe incident has sparked warnings from mountain rescue teams and experts about the dangers of relying on smartphone apps for navigation in the mountains. Chris Lloyd, a member of the Ogwen Rescue team, described the route taken by the couple as \"not a straightforward path\" and emphasized the need for walkers to be aware of the risks and challenges involved.\n\nAndy Simpson, a spokesman for Mountain Rescue England and Wales, also urged walkers to use a map and compass in addition to electronic navigation aids, citing the potential for batteries to run out or signals to be lost.\n\nThe incident serves as a reminder of the importance of proper planning, preparation, and caution when engaging in mountain walking and scrambling activities.",
